  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nIssued 210 PM MDT WED SEP 9 2026\nSouthwest flow ahead of an incoming upper low will keep \nSaturday on the warmer than normal side. Come late Saturday, the\naforementioned upper low will quickly move into our area from \noff the OR coast. This significant pattern shift will bring a \nrapid temperature cooldown, strong westerly winds, and \nwidespread precipitation Saturday afternoon/evening. The \nforecasted winds for late Saturday through early Sunday will be \nparticularly strong, with gusts up to 40 to 50 mph spanning the \nentire southern half of our CWA from southeast OR near the NV \nborder to the lower Snake River Plain. The northern half of the \nCWA will see the bulk of showers/thunderstorms, especially the \ncentral mountains of western ID and eastern OR, while the Magic \nValley stays dry. Showers will continue Sunday morning as \nmoisture wraps around the northern and western flanks of the \neastward-moving upper low. The cooling pattern will drop snow \nlevels down to the 7000-8000 ft range, favoring some light snow \nshowers over high mountain peaks. By late Sunday, our western \nzones should clear, and Sunday night looks clear and cold area- \nwide, except for valley fog early Monday morning. Then sunny and\nwarmer for the rest of Monday before another upper trough digs \nSSW-ward from British Columbia and hangs over the northwest \ncoast. Long-term guidance has the low staying off the coast, \nkeeping our area under a warm southwest flow. As such, Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday will be dry and warm.",
